course: dictionary
lesson: 1438
title: One word, many meanings: carriage
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The word carriage can mean a car of a train, a horse-drawn vehicle, a baby's pram, or the cost of carrying goods. To choose the right one, read the words around it. «In a crowded carriage you take off your backpack and hold it at your feet» is about a train, because crowded people and backpacks fit a train car.
explain: Look for clue words. «I suppose you want to go off in a coach-and-four!» A coach-and-four is pulled by four horses, so this carriage is a grand horse-drawn vehicle. Numbers can be clues too: «A bicycle has 2 wheels, a tricycle 3, a car 4, and a usual train carriage 8.» Here carriage is a train car, and the wheels show how many it has.
explain: Always test your meaning. Swap it into the sentence and check that it still makes sense. If the sentence is about wheels, passengers or horses, the carriage is a vehicle. If it is about money for posting something, carriage means cost.
fact: In the UK, a carriage is one car of a train. In the USA, people usually call it a car.
fact: A carriage can also be a vehicle with wheels, pulled by horses.
fact: A baby carriage is a pram or buggy for a baby.
fact: Carriage can mean the cost of carrying goods, such as the price of posting a parcel.
fact: The same word can have different meanings, so context tells you which one is right.
exercise: «In a crowded carriage you take off your backpack and hold it at your feet.» What is a carriage here? | A pram for a baby | The price of posting a parcel | A horse pulling a cart | *One car of a train
exercise: In the USA, what do people usually call a train carriage? | *A car | A pram | A cart
exercise: «I suppose you want to go off in a coach-and-four!» What pulls a coach-and-four? | *Four horses | A train engine | A bus | Two dogs
test: «A bicycle has 2 wheels, a tricycle 3, a car 4, and a usual train carriage 8.» How many wheels does a usual train carriage have? | 2 | *8 | 4 | 3
test: Which meaning fits «a baby carriage»? | *A pram or buggy for a baby | A train car | A horse-drawn coach | The cost of carrying goods
exam: «The carriage on the parcel cost £5.» What does carriage mean here? | A pram | *The cost of carrying the parcel | A horse and cart | A train car
